## Who decides the next step

<At levels="simple">

An agent is one end of a range. Along it, what changes is who picks the next step: code written in
advance, or the model.

- **A fixed recipe.** Code runs the same steps every time, and the model only writes the text.
- **A fork.** Code sends each question down one of a few set paths, as <ChapterRef to="routing" />
  passes a question to a bigger model when the small one isn't sure.
- **One tool.** The model decides once whether to use a tool, as in <ChapterRef to="tools" />.
- **A loop.** The model picks every next step and when to stop, as in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />.

The further along, the more kinds of task it can take on, but the more it costs and the more one
mistake can lead to another. A good rule is to use the simplest one that does the job.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

An agent is one end of a range, and what changes along it is who decides the next step: code
written in advance, or the model.

- **A fixed chain.** Code runs set steps in order, such as summarize and then translate. The model
  only writes text.
- **A router.** Each request goes down one of a few fixed paths, as <ChapterRef to="routing" />
  passes a question on to a bigger model when the small one seems unsure.
- **One tool call.** The model decides whether to call a tool, and with what, once, as in <ChapterRef to="tools" />.
- **A loop.** The model decides what to do after every result, and how many steps to take, as in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />.

Anthropic calls systems whose path is fixed in code **workflows**, and keeps "agent" for systems
where the model directs its own steps.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" /> A loop suits tasks whose steps
can't be known in advance, but it costs more, takes longer, and lets one wrong step lead to the
next, so their advice is to use the simplest setup that works.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

Their list of workflows also includes running several calls at once and combining their answers,
and having one call check another's work and send it back for changes.<Cite id="anthropic-agents" />

</At>

## Deciding what to look up

<At levels="simple">

In <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />, the harness looked things up once, before the model
answered, using your question as the search. In an agent, search is a tool, so the model decides
when to search and what for: the agent in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" /> searched for just "cat". If the results aren't enough, it
can search again with something it learned from them. Anthropic's research agents start with
short, broad searches and then narrow down.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

In <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />, the harness retrieved once, before the model
answered, with your question as the search. In an agent, search is a tool like any other, so the
model decides whether to search at all and writes the search itself: the agent in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />
searched for just "cat", not the whole task. After reading the results it can search again with something it found,
such as a name the first result mentioned, or stop. ReAct's agents answered questions this way with
search and lookup tools over Wikipedia, reasoning between steps about what to look for
next.<Cite id="yao2023" />

Anthropic contrasts this with retrieval that fetches the most similar passages once: their research
agents search in several steps and adapt to what they find. Left to themselves, though, agents
tended to write long, specific searches that found little, so their prompts tell them to start
short and broad and then narrow down.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" /> Each search also costs a
model call, and an agent can keep searching for something that isn't there.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>

## Tools as code

<At levels="simple">

In <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />, the model asked for one tool, waited for the answer, then asked for the next.
Another way is to let it write a short program that uses the tools, like this made-up one:

```python
rhyme = search_rhymes("cat")[0]
print(count_words(rhyme["text"]))
```

The harness runs the program and gives the model what it printed, so one reply does both steps.
Agents that write programs like this finish more tasks than ones that ask for one tool at a
time.<Cite id="wang2024codeact" /> But now the harness runs code the model wrote, so it has to run it
somewhere safe, cut off from anything that matters.<Cite id="smolagents-secure" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The run in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" /> wrote one tool call per model call: search, wait for the result, then count. Another
design lets the model write its actions as a short program in which every tool is a function, like
this made-up one:

```python
rhyme = search_rhymes("cat")[0]
print(count_words(rhyme["text"]))
```

The harness runs the program and adds whatever it printed to the prompt. One model call now does
what took two, and a program can loop over results, keep them in variables, and combine tools in
ways that one call at a time can't. Across 17 models, writing actions as Python code succeeded up
to 20% more often than writing them as JSON or text in a fixed format.<Cite id="wang2024codeact" />

The catch is that the harness now runs code the model wrote. A plain mistake, or instructions
planted in a tool result (<ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" />), becomes code that runs. So code
agents run it in a sandbox: an interpreter that allows only listed imports and stops runaway
loops, or a separate container or machine with nothing worth harming.<Cite id="smolagents-secure" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

When a program fails, the error message comes back as its result, so the model can read it and fix
the code on its next turn.<Cite id="wang2024codeact" />

</At>

## Plan first, or step by step

<At levels="simple">

The agent in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" /> decided one step at a time: after each result, it chose what to do next. Another
way is to have the model write the whole plan first, with every tool call, and then let the harness
carry it out. A later step refers to an earlier result by a name, like this made-up plan:

```text
Plan: Find the cat rhyme.
#E1 = search_rhymes[cat]
Plan: Count its words.
#E2 = count_words[text of #E1]
```

The harness runs the steps in order, then one last model call reads the plan and the results and
writes the answer. That takes fewer model calls, so it costs less. But the plan can't change its
mind: if the search found two cat rhymes, or none, the plan goes ahead anyway.<Cite id="xu2023rewoo" />
Some agents mix the two: Anthropic's research agent plans first, then decides after each round of
results whether to change course.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The agent in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" /> decided one step at a time: after each result, it chose what to do next. The other
way is to plan first. One model call writes the whole plan, every tool call included, and the
harness carries it out without asking the model again. A later step refers to an earlier result by
a placeholder, as in ReWOO's format; this plan is made up:

```text
Plan: Find the cat rhyme.
#E1 = search_rhymes[cat]
Plan: Count its words.
#E2 = count_words[text of #E1]
```

A final call reads the plan and every result and writes the answer. In the terms of
"Who decides the next step", the model writes its own workflow, then code runs it. Since the model
isn't called after every result, prompts don't pile up: across six public tests, ReWOO used 64%
fewer tokens than a ReAct-style loop with the same tools, and was 4.4 points more accurate on
average.<Cite id="xu2023rewoo" />

The cost is that a plan is written before anything is seen. If the search had found two cat
rhymes, or none, the plan would carry on regardless. ReWOO's authors found plans that rested on
wrong guesses, such as expecting a person's age to appear in a search result, and noted that
exploring an unknown room would need a plan for every possibility.<Cite id="xu2023rewoo" /> So
many agents mix the two. Anthropic's lead research agent writes a plan and saves it outside the
prompt, so it survives if the context fills up, and after each round of results decides whether
to do more or change course.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>

Here are the three designs side by side on the same kind of task, with made-up sizes:

<DesignCompare />

With two tool calls, step by step makes 3 model calls and reads 1,170 tokens; written as code or
planned first, the same task takes 2 calls and 660 or 780 tokens. At six tool calls the loop reads
3,990, the plan 1,140, and the program still 660. What the loop buys with those tokens is a look at
every result before the next step.

<At levels="deep">

The savings come from the square worked out in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />: a loop of
$K$ calls re-reads the instructions, examples, and every earlier result $K$ times, while a plan reads them about twice,
once to plan and once to answer. On HotpotQA, a test of questions that need facts from more than
one Wikipedia article, a ReAct-style agent used 9,795 tokens per question and ReWOO
1,986.<Cite id="xu2023rewoo" />
Planning helps even without tools. Asking GPT-3 to "first understand the problem and devise a plan
to solve the problem", then carry it out, raised its score on grade-school math problems from
56.4% with "Let's think step by step" (<ChapterRef to="steps-and-structure" />) to
58.2%.<Cite id="wang2023plansolve" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Workflows fix the steps in code; an agent lets the model decide them. Use the simplest that
  works.
- In an agent, search is a tool: the model decides when to search and what for, and can search
  again with what it found.
- Some agents write their actions as a short program, so one call can do several steps; that code
  has to run in a sandbox.
- An agent can also plan every step first and then carry the plan out: fewer model calls, but a
  plan can't react to what it finds unless it is checked and revised along the way.

<At levels="deep">

- A planned or coded run reads its instructions about twice; a loop of $K$ calls reads them $K$
  times.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** one agent does every step in one prompt that keeps growing. What if it handed
parts of the job to other agents?
